export const meta = {
  name: 'sharing-chaining-design',
  description: 'Implementation-ready design for metadata-driven key-chaining sharing (two chains, genuine-cutoff revocation, no sidechain)',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Ground', detail: 'verify current metadata/crypto/IPNS-sequence/consumer facts' },
    { title: 'Architect', detail: 'reorganized two-chain node-metadata schema' },
    { title: 'Elaborate', detail: 'flows, write-revoke Ed25519 rotation, resumable rotation, SDK cutover' },
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'adversarial security / correctness / grok-ability' },
    { title: 'Synthesize', detail: 'single implementation-ready design doc' },
  ],
}

const DECISION = `
# Committed direction (do NOT relitigate; design WITHIN this)
CipherBox is a zero-knowledge encrypted IPFS/IPNS vault (server is a dumb relay; never sees plaintext or any unwrapped/derivable key). We are replacing the current DB-driven sharing model (a per-(share,item,keyType) \`share_keys\` table — O(items*recipients) rows; the maintainer calls it a "blurry sidechain") with a METADATA-DRIVEN KEY-CHAINING model.

CHOSEN MODEL: each node's metadata carries the wrapped keys needed to reach its children, sealed so that a holder of the node's key can unwrap the children's keys, recursing down the tree. Sharing = hand the recipient ONE wrapped key at the share-root node (any node — deep folder OR single file). NO per-item DB rows, NO separate lockbox object. The only DB residue is one recipient-ECIES-wrapped root grant per share (the entry point, not a shadow copy of the tree).

EXPLICIT TRADEOFF ACCEPTED: genuine revocation rotates the whole in-scope subtree (O(items) re-encryptions + IPNS republishes). The maintainer accepts the IPNS-publish cost. So optimize for CLARITY and CORRECTNESS, not for cheap revocation.

# Hard requirements
1. ZERO-KNOWLEDGE preserved; client-side crypto only.
2. TWO SEPARATE CHAINS: a READ chain (content-decryption keys) and a WRITE chain (IPNS Ed25519 signing keys). A read-only share gets ONLY the read chain — write/IPNS-private material must never be transmitted or derivable on the read path.
3. SELECTIVE DEEP SHARES: any node can be a share root, including a single file. (=> a file must be readable from a grant at the file node alone — consider whether FileMetadata must self-encrypt under its own fileKey rather than the parent folderKey.)
4. GENUINE-CUTOFF revocation = rotate-from-node-down. Must be RESUMABLE and IDEMPOTENT (rotating ~1e6 nodes is not atomic; a crash mid-rotation must not leave a revoked recipient reading the un-rotated tail; rotate the share-root entry point FIRST).
5. UNIFY the revocation-gap class: delete, rename-overwrite-destination, and move-OUT-of-a-shared-scope all become the SAME rule — "a node leaving a recipient's reachable scope ⇒ rotate that node's key." Show this collapses the bug class we keep patching one mutation-path at a time.
6. WRITE revocation is the nasty part: rotating content keys does NOT stop a recipient who cached a node's IPNS PRIVATE (Ed25519) key from publishing to that k51 name forever. Genuine write-revoke ⇒ new Ed25519 keypair → new k51 name → rewrite the PARENT's pointer to the child → republish, cascading. This RACES the project's known IPNS sequence-number / folderTree-desync class (stale-sequence 409s). Design for it explicitly.
7. AEAD domain-separation: every sealed key entry must be bound (AAD or per-entry derived key via HKDF) to its node id + key-role + generation, so a malicious relay cannot transplant a sealed blob between slots.
8. CLEAN BREAK: no data migration, no existing users. Reorganize the metadata schema freely for clarity — the maintainer explicitly invited "reorganize the metadata as you see fit to make this entire structure easier to grok." Unifying Folder/File/Root metadata into one clear Node model with a type discriminator is in-bounds and encouraged if it's genuinely cleaner.

# Files to read for ground truth (cite file:line)
docs/METADATA_SCHEMAS.md, docs/FILESYSTEM_SPECIFICATION.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/AUTHENTICATION_ARCHITECTURE.md
packages/core/src (metadata/types), packages/crypto/src (primitives: wrapKey/ECIES, sealAesGcm/unsealAesGcm, deriveContextKey/HKDF, ed25519 sign/verify, generateRandomBytes; confirm AAD support)
IPNS derivation + sequence: grep deriveIpnsName / publicKeyFromIpnsName / sequenceNumber; apps/api/src/ipns/*; apps/web/src/services/ipns.service.ts
shares: apps/api/src/shares/* ; apps/web/src/services/share.service.ts (note executeLazyRotation:602 rotates ONLY the share-root folderKey — confirmed unsound) and folder.service.ts
consumers: crates/fuse/src/metadata.rs, publish.rs, replay.rs, write_ops/implementation/{delete,rename}.rs ; packages/sdk/src/share/* ; packages/sdk-core/*
`

phase('Ground')

const ground = await parallel([
  () => agent(`${DECISION}

# Your job: METADATA + CRYPTO ground truth
Document, with file:line citations: (a) the EXACT current FolderMetadata / FileMetadata / RootMetadata / VersionEntry field layouts and what is encrypted under what key; (b) precisely how child keys are sealed today (the owner-ECIES-wrapped per-entry fields) and where the AES-GCM blob boundary is; (c) the crypto primitives packages/crypto actually exposes — signatures for wrapKey/unwrapKey, sealAesGcm/unsealAesGcm (does it accept AAD?), deriveContextKey/HKDF (domain-sep), ed25519 keygen/sign/verify, generateRandomBytes; (d) is a FILE currently decryptable with only its fileKey, or does it require the parent folderKey (i.e. is FileMetadata sealed under fileKey or parent folderKey)? This determines whether single-file shares need a schema change. Return a tight factual brief, ~400 words.`, { label: 'ground:metadata-crypto', phase: 'Ground', effort: 'medium' }),
  () => agent(`${DECISION}

# Your job: IPNS NAME/SEQUENCE + SHARE-FLOW ground truth
Document, with file:line: (a) how an IPNS name (k51) is derived from the Ed25519 keypair, and the inverse (publicKeyFromIpnsName); (b) the IPNS record sequence-number flow — who assigns it, how stale-sequence 409s arise, and the folderTree-desync class (web Zustand store vs SDK folderTree vs published record); (c) the CURRENT share data model end-to-end: shares + share_keys columns, createShare, addShareKeys/reWrapForRecipients, and the revocation path (checkPendingRotation, executeLazyRotation:602, completeShareRotation) — confirm exactly what rotates and what does NOT; (d) how IPNS publishing is relayed/throttled (someguy, 2 PUTs per publish, TEE 6h republish). Return a tight factual brief, ~400 words.`, { label: 'ground:ipns-shares', phase: 'Ground', effort: 'medium' }),
  () => agent(`${DECISION}

# Your job: CONSUMER ground truth (FUSE + SDK)
Document, with file:line: how the metadata is CONSUMED for (a) read/navigate (FUSE resolve + decrypt; SDK navigation), (b) write/publish (FUSE publish.rs / metadata.rs spawn_metadata_publish; sequence handling; the strict IPNS verify cutover), (c) the delete and rename-over-destination paths (crates/fuse/src/write_ops/implementation/{delete,rename}.rs) and the recently-added revoke_shares_blocking hook, (d) how a cross-folder move currently re-encrypts file metadata (spawn_file_meta_reencrypt). Flag every place that would have to change if child keys are sealed under the PARENT node key (read chain) + a separate WRITE chain, and every place that assumes a stable k51 name (which write-revoke rotation would break). Return a tight factual brief, ~400 words.`, { label: 'ground:consumers', phase: 'Ground', effort: 'medium' }),
])

const groundBrief = ground.filter(Boolean).map((g, i) => `## Ground brief ${i + 1}
${g}`).join('

')

phase('Architect')

const schema = await agent(
  `${DECISION}

# Verified current-state ground truth
${groundBrief}

# Your job: ARCHITECT the reorganized metadata + key model
Design the NEW node-metadata schema for the key-chaining model, prioritizing GROK-ABILITY (the maintainer explicitly wants the structure easier to understand). Deliver field-level:
1. The node metadata object(s). Decide whether to unify Folder/File/Root into one Node schema with a type discriminator, or keep them separate — justify which is clearer. Show exactly what is plaintext, what is AES-GCM-sealed and under which key, and what each child entry carries.
2. The READ chain: how a node seals each child's content key so a holder of THIS node's read key can unwrap them. Include the AAD/domain-sep binding (req 7). Ensure a single file is readable from a grant at the file node alone (req 3) — state whether FileMetadata must move to self-encrypt under its own fileKey.
3. The WRITE chain: how a node seals each child's IPNS Ed25519 PRIVATE key under THIS node's write key, kept strictly separate from the read chain (req 2).
4. The share grant: the single recipient-ECIES-wrapped root key(s) per share (read-only = read root only; writer = read + write roots), and where it lives (DB shares row). Show the DB shrinks to O(recipients).
5. A 'generation' / key-version field per node to support resumable rotation and stale-read rejection.
Return clean markdown with JSON schema sketches. Be decisive; this schema is the foundation everything else builds on. Use proper markdown headings (not bold-as-heading) and blank lines around code fences/lists.`,
  { label: 'architect:schema', phase: 'Architect', effort: 'high' }
)

phase('Elaborate')

const slices = await parallel([
  () => agent(`${DECISION}

# The architected schema (build on it EXACTLY; do not redesign it)
${schema}

# Your job: FLOWS + Big-O
Give step-by-step client-side flows, each with Big-O and IPNS-republish count at 1e6 items / 10 recipients, for: share-create (read-only and write), recipient read/navigate into a deep child, recipient write/publish (writer share), add-item to a shared subtree, move-node WITHIN scope, move-node OUT of scope (req 5), rename-over-existing-destination (req 5), delete (req 5), and key freshness on add. Show explicitly how delete / rename-overwrite / move-out collapse into the single "node leaving scope ⇒ rotate" rule. Return markdown with a per-op cost table + the flow steps.`, { label: 'elab:flows', phase: 'Elaborate', effort: 'high' }),
  () => agent(`${DECISION}

# The architected schema (build on it EXACTLY)
${schema}

# Your job: WRITE-REVOCATION via Ed25519 keypair rotation (req 6)
Design genuine write-revocation. Cover: generating a new Ed25519 keypair → new k51 name for a node; rewriting the PARENT's pointer to the child; cascading this up to the share root; and republishing. CRITICAL: handle the IPNS SEQUENCE-NUMBER race / folderTree-desync class (stale-sequence 409s) — when a node's k51 changes, sequence resets and parent pointers must update atomically-enough that a concurrent reader/writer/TEE-republisher never wedges. Specify ordering, how the new name is announced, how the old name is abandoned (and whether the TEE republish schedule must be updated/cancelled), and how a write-recipient who cached the OLD ipns private key is provably cut off. Identify failure modes and the recovery story. Return markdown.`, { label: 'elab:write-revoke', phase: 'Elaborate', effort: 'high' }),
  () => agent(`${DECISION}

# The architected schema (build on it EXACTLY)
${schema}

# Your job: RESUMABLE + IDEMPOTENT subtree rotation job (req 4)
Design the rotation that genuinely cuts a revoked recipient at ~1e6-node scale. Cover: ordering (share-root FIRST so the entry point is severed even if the tail lags), checkpointing/progress persistence, idempotency (re-running after a crash converges; double-rotation is safe), detection of a half-rotated/mixed-generation subtree, how the 'generation' field gates stale reads during the window, where the job runs (client? must the owner be online? can it resume across sessions?), and how it interacts with concurrent mutations and the TEE 6h republisher. Give the bounded-exposure-window analysis and a lazy rotate-on-next-write variant. Return markdown.`, { label: 'elab:rotation-job', phase: 'Elaborate', effort: 'high' }),
  () => agent(`${DECISION}

# The architected schema (build on it EXACTLY)
${schema}

# Your job: DOWNSTREAM / SDK CUTOVER plan (clean break, no migration)
Map the implementation blast radius across packages/crypto, packages/core (metadata schema), packages/sdk-core, packages/sdk (share + navigation), apps/api (delete share_keys table + endpoints; what the relay still stores), apps/web (replace unsound executeLazyRotation; share UI), crates/fuse (read/write chains; the unified rotate-on-scope-exit rule; the strict IPNS verify path). For each: what changes, rough invasiveness, and the ordering of the cutover so the monorepo stays buildable. Call out the TOP risks and a concrete TEST STRATEGY — especially how to test the O(items) rotation storm AND its partial-failure/resumability (the maintainer noted throughput is easy to test but correctness-under-crash is the real risk). Return markdown.`, { label: 'elab:cutover', phase: 'Elaborate', effort: 'high' }),
])

const sliceBundle = slices.filter(Boolean).map((s, i) => {
  const titles = ['Flows + Big-O', 'Write-revocation (Ed25519 rotation)', 'Resumable rotation job', 'Downstream/SDK cutover']
  return `## ${titles[i] || ('Slice ' + i)}
${s}`
}).join('

')

phase('Review')

const reviews = await parallel([
  () => agent(`You are an adversarial CRYPTO/ZERO-KNOWLEDGE reviewer. Find every flaw in this key-chaining sharing design: (a) any path where a READ-ONLY recipient can obtain write/IPNS-private capability (read/write chain bleed); (b) anything the relay/server learns it shouldn't, or any key derivable server-side; (c) missing/weak AAD domain-sep letting a malicious relay transplant sealed blobs between node slots; (d) forward/backward-secrecy gaps after revocation given cached keys/content; (e) KDF reuse, nonce reuse, or generation-field forgery. Cite the specific mechanism in the design. Severity-tag findings (blocker/major/minor).

# Committed design
${DECISION}

# Schema
${schema}

# Slices
${sliceBundle}

Return markdown findings + one-line verdict.`, { label: 'review:security', phase: 'Review', effort: 'high' }),
  () => agent(`You are an adversarial CORRECTNESS/REVOCATION reviewer. Pressure-test: (a) does the resumable rotation REALLY cut a revoked recipient if it crashes mid-run — trace the worst-case interleaving; (b) the IPNS sequence-number / k51-rotation races in write-revoke — can a concurrent writer, reader, or the TEE republisher wedge or resurrect an old name; (c) does the unified "node leaving scope ⇒ rotate" rule actually close delete / rename-overwrite / move-out without leaving an orphaned grant; (d) add-item correctness (existing recipients gain access without re-wrapping everything); (e) any half-rotated/mixed-generation state a reader could exploit. Find gaps and unhandled edge cases. Cite the design. Severity-tag.

# Committed design
${DECISION}

# Schema
${schema}

# Slices
${sliceBundle}

Return markdown findings + one-line verdict.`, { label: 'review:correctness', phase: 'Review', effort: 'high' }),
  () => agent(`You are a SIMPLICITY / GROK-ABILITY reviewer. The maintainer explicitly wants this structure "easier to grok." Critique the schema and flows for ACCIDENTAL complexity: redundant fields, two concepts that should be one, special-cases that should be general rules, anything a new engineer would trip on. Is unifying Folder/File/Root into one Node model actually clearer, or did it add ceremony? Propose concrete simplifications that do NOT sacrifice the security/correctness requirements. Also sanity-check naming against the repo's terminology standards (folderKey, fileKey, ipnsName, keyEpoch, etc.). Cite specifics.

# Committed design
${DECISION}

# Schema
${schema}

# Slices
${sliceBundle}

Return markdown: prioritized simplifications + one-line verdict on whether the design is as grok-able as it should be.`, { label: 'review:simplicity', phase: 'Review', effort: 'high' }),
])

const reviewBundle = reviews.filter(Boolean).map((r, i) => {
  const titles = ['Security/zero-knowledge', 'Correctness/revocation', 'Simplicity/grok']
  return `## Review: ${titles[i] || i}
${r}`
}).join('

')

phase('Synthesize')

const designDoc = await agent(
  `${DECISION}

A schema was architected, four design slices were elaborated on it, and three adversarial reviews (security, correctness, simplicity) critiqued the whole. Your job: produce ONE implementation-ready design document, integrating the slices and APPLYING the review fixes (resolve blockers/majors; note any you intentionally defer with rationale).

# Architected schema
${schema}

# Slices
${sliceBundle}

# Adversarial reviews (apply the fixes)
${reviewBundle}

# Deliver this exact structure in clean markdown
1. Overview & rationale (why chaining-on-metadata, the no-sidechain win, the accepted revocation tradeoff) — short.
2. The reorganized metadata schema (field-level; the unified/clearer Node model; read chain vs write chain; AAD binding; generation field).
3. The share grant (DB residue = O(recipients) root grants) and read-only vs writer.
4. Flows with Big-O + IPNS-republish counts (share, navigate, publish, add, move-in, move-out, rename-overwrite, delete) and the unified rotate-on-scope-exit rule.
5. Write-revocation via Ed25519 rotation (k51 change, parent-pointer rewrite, sequence-race handling, TEE republish update, proof of cutoff).
6. Resumable/idempotent subtree rotation job (ordering, checkpointing, generation gating, crash recovery, lazy variant, exposure-window).
7. Downstream blast radius + cutover ordering (per package) and test strategy (incl. partial-failure/resumability testing).
8. Open questions for the maintainer.
Markdownlint-friendly: proper headings (no bold-as-heading), blank lines around code fences and lists. Be decisive and concrete — this doc is what the maintainer reads to greenlight implementation and what planning will be built from.`,
  { label: 'synthesize:design-doc', phase: 'Synthesize', effort: 'high' }
)

return { designDoc }

